{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB09559 - MONDO_8903",
  "verbalization": "Necitumumab, a monoclonal antibody, is implicated in the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Necitumumab is associated with NSCLC via the indications of Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab, both of which are immune checkpoint inhibitors. These agents target PD-1 or PD-L1, which are critical in regulating T-cell activity and tumor immune escape. The presence of a direct indication from Necitumumab to NSCLC, along with the indirect pathways through Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab, indicates a potential role in the immune-mediated suppression of tumor growth. The shared indication of NSCLC across these pathways suggests a possible mechanism involving immune checkpoint inhibition, which could be a key driver of the observed relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of immune checkpoint modulation in the context of NSCLC, and the evidence supports the hypothesis that Necitumumab may contribute to the treatment of this disease through such mechanisms.",
